      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat a frying pan over a medium-high heat. Add the pork belly slices and cook for 2 to 3 minutes per side until lightly coloured. Set aside."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Pour the dashi stock into a large saucepan and bring to a gentle simmer over a medium heat."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the soy sauce, mirin and sake. Stir and taste. Adjust seasoning if needed. This seasoned broth is the kakejiru."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "udon"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "noodles to the broth. Simmer gently for 2 minutes until heated through."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Divide the noodles and broth between four bowls. Lay the pork slices on top. Add narutomaki if using. Scatter over the spring onions and serve straight away."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "attrs": {
          "items": [
            {
              "symptom": "Broth tastes flat",
              "cause": "The dashi may be weak or the seasoning is too low.",
              "fix": "Add another tablespoon of soy sauce and a pinch of salt. A splash more mirin lifts the sweetness too."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Noodles are too soft and falling apart",
              "cause": "They were cooked too long or the broth was at a rolling boil.",
              "fix": "Next time add the noodles off the boil and warm them for just 1 to 2 minutes. Fresh udon needs very little time."
            },
            {
              "symptom": "Pork is tough",
              "cause": "The slices were too thick or cooked at too high a heat for too long.",
              "fix": "Use slices no thicker than 3 mm. A short, hot sear is all they need before going into the hot broth."
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Niku udon comes from the Kansai region of Japan. It is a popular weeknight bowl across Osaka and Kyoto. The word niku means meat. The rich, golden"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "kakejiru"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "broth is built on"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "dashi"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": ". It is lighter than ramen but deeply satisfying."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}